# vendor/ — PACBench-owned isolated Hermes (W1)

## Reproduce the current runtime

From a checkout of this branch on Linux x86-64, run:

```sh
python3 -m pacbench.bootstrap --install --start-services
```

Requirements: Python 3.11+, `uv`, Git, GNU tar with `zstd`, Node 22.21+ with npm, and a working
local Docker daemon. Keep the inherited HTTPS proxy and CA trust. Run as the
normal user with writable workspace and home directories. Stock Ollama creates
its native identity in `~/.ollama`; a sandbox that makes the home read-only
needs a normal outside-sandbox setup invocation. The installer never reads,
creates or replaces the task's private ledger or needs an inference API key.

The default invocation without `--install` prints a plan. `--components hermes
honcho` selects a subset; `--check` verifies installed identities offline.
Pinned Hermes includes the Mem0 and other provider clients and the clean Memcal
0.8.11 checkout. Separate environments install Hindsight, OpenViking, ReMe,
Cognee and Honcho. MemOS and Supermemory assets are hash-verified. The clock
uses the existing reproducible Docker build. Embeddings install a pinned
CPU Ollama runtime, native Nomic model and offline MiniLM files. A temporary
loopback Ollama server downloads the model and checks a 768-dimensional local
embedding, then stops. `--start-services` additionally leaves the verified runtime
ready at `127.0.0.1:11435`, checking its version, model identity and actual local
embedding. Existing mismatched services are refused. No paid model requests or
source ingestion occur.

Existing source trees at a different revision, untracked files, or changes
other than Hermes's reviewed isolation patch fail closed. Preserve and move
those directories aside explicitly before retrying; the installer never resets
them. Downloads can require several GB. Top-level provider releases, source
commits and asset hashes are pinned. Reviewed Python patch versions and selected
backend dependencies are constrained by `vendor/runtime-locks`; MemOS uses its
reviewed npm lock. These snapshots constrain the selected runtime without
installing optional or development packages.
Installation identity is not native processing or recall qualification.

Example saved cloud-environment setup, with this repository already selected
at the desired review commit:

```sh
set -eu
cd /workspace/PACBench
python3 -m pacbench.bootstrap --install --start-services
python3 -m pacbench.bootstrap --check
```

Before paid execution, restore the **original** private directory
`/workspace/shared/pacbench-openrouter-pizza-day-20261112` using the environment's
private persistence/handoff mechanism. It must contain `ledger.json` and
`authorization.json`, including all historical unknowns, reservations and
external allocations. Never put it in Git, a setup script, or rebuild it from a
checkpoint total. A fresh code checkout alone does not carry spending history.
Keep model credentials in environment settings. Missing accounting blocks paid
execution independently of a successful bootstrap.

Use the route-specific free preflight to avoid confusing missing historical
Muse configuration with the authorized OpenRouter route:

```sh
TMPDIR=/var/tmp python3 -m pacbench.preflight --inference-route openrouter \
  --prepared artifacts/pizza-week-hybrid-stream-v7-20261113/week.json --all-providers
```

Start the installed embedding server in the execution environment before a run:

```sh
OLLAMA_MODELS="$PWD/vendor/ollama/models" OLLAMA_HOST=127.0.0.1:11435 \
  vendor/ollama/bin/ollama serve
```

This process stays in the foreground; keep it in a separate terminal or managed
process. Native adapters own their other services. Select `TMPDIR=/var/tmp`
for recall and tests because Hermes must run outside Git. To run the offline
suite, install the test-only dependencies, then execute:

```sh
uv pip install --python vendor/venv-hermes-isolated/bin/python pytest PyYAML
TMPDIR=/var/tmp vendor/venv-hermes-isolated/bin/python -m pytest -q
```

The source pins and ordinary backend settings below remain authoritative.

**Fresh native trials require the pinned setup and original shared accounting.**
Use the reviewed cohort launcher below only with an authorized spending cap.
The isolated Hermes v0.23.1 integration has
passed a free synthetic local gateway/CLI canary and a separate direct HTTPS
check. Each turn owns and gracefully stops its native gateway. The current
Honcho `honcho-wall-clock-v5` protocol uses a shared-offset Python clock for
Hermes/API/deriver and the reproducible preload library for PostgreSQL only;
OpenSSL and monotonic timers retain real time. Gateway and CLI adoption is bound
to each process identity and turn nonce. The bounded natural startup interval
and native submission/admission guards are documented in the provider map;
CLI launch time is never reported as actual question dispatch. Long homes use
Hermes's authenticated native short-socket pointer, not a guessed socket path. The native system date line supplies a
date, not exact current time. Historical clock receipts retain their original
protocols and cannot resume as v5. These checks establish transport behavior,
not model quality or native provider processing. Keep the persistent OpenRouter
task ledger and its existing uncertain holds; never initialize a replacement.
Every arm must pass native preflight and processing barriers before recall.
The unrelated Muse route
remains blocked if its historical ledger is missing.

This release supports Python 3.14 for normal runtime use. Its lock explicitly
overrides `browser-harness==0.1.13`'s `pillow==12.3.0` requirement with
`pillow==13.0.0`; Hermes PM recognizes this intentional metadata mismatch.
Use the upstream locked/PM installation, not an ordinary unconstrained pip
install. The documented source developer activation selects the `all` extra:

  export HERMES_HOME="$(mktemp -d)"
  export HERMES_RUNTIME_DIR="$HERMES_HOME/tools"
  cd vendor/hermes-isolated
  source ./activate
  hermes pm doctor

Run this in an isolated shell from the PACBench root. Upstream documents this
as a runtime-only developer setup, not the full user-facing installer. PM
activation itself has not been verified here; native provider recall also
remains unverified.

The isolated target environment was built from the upstream lock using the
`all` extra, then the selected client requirements and Memcal were installed.
Reproduce this non-PM-managed environment from the PACBench root, with Python
3.14.9 available and `/path/to/memcal` set to the intended checkout:

  export UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT="$PWD/vendor/venv-hermes-isolated"
  (cd vendor/hermes-isolated && uv sync --locked --no-default-groups --extra all --python /path/to/python3.14)
  uv pip install --python "$UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT/bin/python" -r vendor/hermes-isolated-extra-requirements.txt -e /path/to/memcal

The current stable-provider cohort uses Memcal 0.8.11 from an isolated checkout.
Set `PACBENCH_MEMCAL_SRC` to that clean release checkout when its location differs
from the default sibling `memcal-stable-20261113`; source verification requires
commit `e67b8d912484b5b4317dafcff4d60fc975194d8e`. Original development
installations do not determine current runtime versions. Install the additional
client requirements after syncing Hermes and verify the actual distributions.
Hermes v0.23.1 is still the current stable release. Its upstream Pillow lock
override is retained. Offline imports and tests alone do not qualify recall.

- `HERMES_PIN`: pinned upstream remote + full commit SHA (tracked).
- `hermes-isolated/`: git clone of https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent.git
  at that SHA plus exactly `patches/hermes-isolated.diff`; integrity tests reject other edits.
- `venv-hermes-isolated/`: isolated venv, editable install of that clone
  (gitignored). Use with a scratch home, never `~/.hermes`:
  `HERMES_HOME=$(mktemp -d) vendor/venv-hermes-isolated/bin/hermes --version`

The client versions in `hermes-isolated-extra-requirements.txt` document the
target environment; they do not yet establish a PM-managed provisioning
workflow. Install Memcal with its declared dependencies, including pytypedstream,
when provisioning its provider; a source-only import is insufficient.
Run native servers in their own environments: OpenViking requires MCP <2 whereas
Hermes uses MCP 2. Keep Hindsight's API dependencies separate from Hermes' pinned
clients too; its older API releases required an incompatible cryptography version.
The Hindsight SDK stays in Hermes; provisioning starts/stops its real self-hosted
daemon using `vendor/venv-hindsight/bin/python`:

  uv venv --python 3.12 vendor/venv-hindsight
  uv pip install --python vendor/venv-hindsight/bin/python torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
  uv pip install --python vendor/venv-hindsight/bin/python hindsight-all==1.2.1

Use the official CPU PyTorch wheel on Linux CPU hosts; the default GPU wheel pulls
several GB of unused CUDA dependencies. These are hardware/transport installations,
not changes to provider extraction prompts or processing defaults.
Hindsight's server version is separate from Hermes' pinned client version:
the older 0.6.1 API does not implement the native Meta model adapter.

Honcho uses the upstream manual self-hosting checkout at commit
`70c67624169683b9e265517aec95a51b8077cabc` (3.6.1), installed with
`uv sync --frozen --no-dev` in `vendor/honcho-local`. Keep its native batching
and output defaults. Hermes pins the published `honcho-ai==2.9.0` client. A release update does not establish successful ingestion;
verify a small native import and Hermes recall before larger runs.

## Hermes catalog memory providers

The pinned Hermes release moves these providers out of its source tree. Its reviewed
catalog entries identify the following plugin sources and commits (these pins describe
plugin code, not the independently provisioned native servers):

| Provider | Source path | Catalog commit |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mem0 | `mem0ai/mem0/integrations/hermes-plugin-mem0` | `43849c6e9d4d773b83a8144b6373aedc67241a5d` |
| Honcho | `plastic-labs/honcho/hermes-plugin-honcho` | `894b88321872e01702a1aa638ddfdde6ad60f173` |
| Hindsight | `vectorize-io/hindsight/hindsight-integrations/hermes` | `f14cc1eb5624b0374cb4d82768eb2e0b9c21ae24` |
| Supermemory | `supermemoryai/hermes-supermemory` | `91405dd00fdf2a0b3515b25c25557598f6793299` |
| OpenViking | `volcengine/OpenViking/examples/hermes-plugin` | `e855796c83f9eb575d9b0f3082926660730f6a63` |

For fresh homes the stable-provider cohort uses Hermes's documented source form
with explicit `--ref --no-deps --no-enable`. `pacbench/catalog_plugins.py`
records selected upstream source revisions separately from the historical
Hermes catalog above. Plugins come from Mem0 2.2.1, Honcho 3.6.1, Hindsight 1.2.1
and OpenViking 0.5.4.1 releases. Supermemory has no standalone plugin tags or
releases; its current declared plugin 2.0.0 is pinned to full revision
`a8f360166419958ce77b876c69f7be3bf0716d67`, requiring SDK 5.0.1.
The published local server remains server-v0.0.9. Native v5 document import,
namespace reads, processing and dreaming completion must all be verified.
server-v0.0.9 omits `dreamingStatus` from v5 document responses. The runner
observes its supported native v3 list/get API read-only, binding the namespace,
public/native IDs, unchanged source, metadata and actual consolidation phase.
Indexing completion alone remains insufficient. Actor SDK/plugin versions stay
current; the observer neither resubmits source nor changes native processing.

Cognee 1.6.8 runs in a private server environment with the unchanged 1.4.3 native
HTTP integration. Its automatic local installer still selects 1.6.6; PACBench
manually provisions the newer local server and records that distinction.
ReMe uses stable v0.4.2.2, including its `core` extra, rather than development
0.4.2.3. Install its shipped Hermes integration from the same release. Preserve
its default resource watcher, extraction, indexing and result-hook witnesses.

MemOS's native local plugin is a separate release from the root Python package.
Install official npm `@memtensor/memos-local-plugin@2.0.21` under
`vendor/memos-local-plugin/`, preserving the verified `release.tgz` and extracted
`package/`. `MEMOS_LOCAL_PIN.json` binds the archive; the harness compares every
shipped file before loading it. Install dependencies with Node's inherited proxy
support (`NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1`). Production installation used `--omit=dev
--omit=optional --legacy-peer-deps` because unused DeepSeek Harness development
peers conflict upstream. No runtime source files are rewritten. The native
Hermes bridge, public core bootstrap and raw-document HTTP API remain unchanged.
The bootstrap additionally installs the Linux x64 Sharp/libvips runtime packages
required by Transformers for native text embeddings; npm marks these optional,
but omitting them can break the import. It verifies an offline 384-dimensional
embedding through MemOS's shipped embedder before declaring assets ready.

Each new cohort needs fresh native processing, context injection and tool checks.
Fresh homes disable lazy installs after verified plugin installation. Service
readiness and dependent-write processing receipts remain required; version
updates never imply successful ingestion or recall.

## Rebuild this cohort in another session

Runtime directories above are ignored installations and are not carried by a Git
clone. Rebuild them from the recorded releases; do not copy private provider
homes, model credentials or old trial stores. Code and the frozen Week are on
`upgrade/stable-providers-20261113`; use a reviewed full commit, then follow the
existing Hermes lock/patch commands above. The source inventory is
`artifacts/provider-stable-upgrade-20261113/installed-components.json`.

The qualified Linux x86-64 interpreters were Python 3.14.9 for Hermes, 3.13.5 for
Honcho, and 3.12.14 for Hindsight/OpenViking/Cognee/ReMe. Preserve separate venvs.
Install the following without starting model work:

- Clone Hermes at `vendor/HERMES_PIN`, apply `patches/hermes-isolated.diff`, sync
  its locked `all` extra into `vendor/venv-hermes-isolated`, then install the
  extra client requirements and clean Memcal 0.8.11 checkout described above.
- Provision `vendor/venv-hindsight` with official CPU PyTorch and
  `hindsight-all==1.2.1`; `vendor/venv-openviking` with `openviking==0.5.4.1`;
  and `vendor/venv-cognee` with `cognee==1.6.8`. The installed ReMe runtime used
  `agentscope==2.0.11` as well as the stable ReMe core extra.
- Clone `plastic-labs/honcho` at
  `70c67624169683b9e265517aec95a51b8077cabc` into `vendor/honcho-local` and
  `uv sync --frozen --no-dev` with Python 3.13.5. PostgreSQL used
  `pgvector/pgvector@sha256:88164492558c9773a1e17a8305abaca7863e05b3d8bbcdb792774890a2047e06`.
- Clone `agentscope-ai/ReMe` at
  `36c23674ad8c599dae20b0687af40acd8ecb990e` into `vendor/reme`, and install
  its `core` extra into `vendor/venv-reme`. Clone `topoteretes/cognee-integrations`
  at `a176248914fdb32a78354854b300174203b15a5c` into `vendor/cognee-integrations`.
  Native plugin setup is performed by the existing adapters in fresh run homes.
- Download the official Supermemory `server-v0.0.9` release asset
  `supermemory-server-linux-x64` into `vendor/supermemory-local`, verify SHA-256
  `d7987ecbf2b024c6de9d167becbcfcaabce447169b41d6f56db86f87d5187984`, mark it
  executable, and retain an `installation.json` matching the inventory record.
  Install MemOS's verified npm archive and dependencies as described above.
- The original machine used Node 24.22.1, uv 0.13.5 and Ollama 0.40.16. Provision
  local `nomic-embed-text` at `127.0.0.1:11435`; its observed model digest was
  `0a109f422b47e3a30ba2b10eca18548e944e8a23073ee3f3e947efcf3c45e59f`.
  Check an actual 768-dimensional embedding before initializing providers.
- Build `vendor/libfaketime/install-runtime-v4.sh` using its pinned container and
  verify the library digest in `BUILD.md`. Install the hash-pinned Python clock
  wheels with `vendor/python-clock/install-runtime.py` into the selected existing
  Python venvs. Keep TLS and monotonic deadlines on real time.

These are recorded working pins and a reconstruction recipe, not proof that a
fresh installation succeeded. Verify actual dependencies, plugin imports,
clock instrumentation, TLS and local embeddings in the destination. Configure
`OPENROUTER_API_KEY` through the managed environment settings; never put its value
in Git, handoff text or the accounting archive. No service credentials are needed
from the old native stores.

Transfer original accounting separately with `python -m
pacbench.accounting_transfer export ... --destination-thread <thread-id>`.
It freezes source paid admission and exports only private JSON accounting data.
Restore the verified archive in the destination with its expected SHA-256 and
matching thread ID. `restore ... --activate` changes only ownership metadata;
all calls, uncertain bounds, outstanding reservations and allocations are kept.
Never overwrite existing accounting or run both copies. The source stays frozen.
The archive belongs in private storage, never this public Git repository.

The frozen Week imports and audits without model calls. After destination setup,
original accounting restoration, applicable approval and integrity checks pass,
`scripts/run_reviewed_week_cohort.py --output runs/<new-name>` launches the nine
isolated local arms. It uses the original $20 task cap, exact DeepInfra Flex
route, native questions and shared frozen source. Do not run Week in the source
session during this handoff.

Failed-arm retries use a new output directory and `--providers`, for example
`python scripts/run_reviewed_week_cohort.py --providers mem0 hindsight honcho --output runs/week-retry --concurrency 3`.
The launcher supports 1–9 concurrent arms (`--concurrency 8` for eight remaining
providers); each keeps isolated native stores and shares the same atomic spending
ledger. Concurrent trials do not support latency rankings.
Preserve successful original arms and select replacements explicitly when
publishing; do not copy failed stores into a fresh attempt.
Use `--import-only` on the same launcher to qualify the entire frozen source
without sending benchmark questions or invoking the judge. Every batch must
finish native processing before the next is imported; a failure stops that arm.
`processing-complete` means its ingestion audit passed, not that semantic fact
review is finished. Keep these full-week qualification stores separate from
chronological benchmark stores to prevent future-source exposure.
The reviewed retry launcher gives the benchmark judge a 600-second HTTP wall
deadline (`--judge-timeout-s 600`), recorded separately from its unchanged low
reasoning setting. This avoids cancelling otherwise active streamed judgments
at 180 seconds. Recall and recovery receive a separately recorded 600-second
harness deadline (`--recall-timeout-s 600`). Neither changes provider or Hermes
model parameters. A timed-out turn remains ungraded, including when incomplete
clock evidence also fails the trace checks; it is not labeled a forbidden-tool
attempt solely because the process was interrupted.

For an ended OpenRouter request with a recorded generation ID but missing usage,
`python -m pacbench.openrouter_reconcile LEDGER_REQUEST_ID` fetches free generation
and model-catalog receipts, stores them in the private accounting directory,
and settles only validated actual usage. Requests without a generation ID remain
held. No inference call, budget reset, or automatic replay is performed.

Use `--detach` when a reviewed cohort must keep running while a terminal or chat
turn ends, for example:

```bash
python scripts/run_reviewed_week_cohort.py --import-only --detach \
  --providers memcal mem0 hindsight openviking honcho reme cognee memos \
  --concurrency 8 --output runs/week-import-new
```

This creates a private PID receipt and controller log beside the output directory.
`launched` confirms process creation only; inspect the log and provider receipts
for preflight, processing and verification results. A second launch with the same
output name is refused. Continue to preserve previously completed providers.

Measured import qualification with bounded cleanup has one entry point:

```bash
vendor/venv-hermes-isolated/bin/python -m pacbench.measured_import \
  --output runs/week-import-measured --concurrency 2 --timeout-s 3600 --detach
```

It verifies the reviewed corpus and original shared accounting, creates fresh
stores for the selected providers (all nine by default), and invokes only native
import. It sends no benchmark questions and no judge calls. Use `--providers`
to select affected arms. Choose concurrency explicitly: upstream rate limits and
simultaneous conservative cost reservations can make nine arms impractical.
This does not change provider model settings, batching or prompts.

Cancel the entire measured cohort with `touch runs/week-import-measured/STOP`.
The supervisor requests cooperative stopping, then enforces a bounded real-clock
grace period against verified run-owned processes and containers. Its default
one-hour per-provider limit includes provisioning, imports and native readiness;
it does not classify a five-minute import as failed. Cancellation and timeouts
preserve stores, failed receipts and all accounting holds. An HTTP 429 creates
the cohort STOP atomically at settlement and blocks new admissions across its
projects, preventing automatic SDK retry storms; it does not settle that charge.
Use a new reviewed attempt after investigating the rate limit.

Inspect `measured-cohort.json`, `<provider>.lifecycle.json` and
`<provider>.metrics.json`. Private ownership receipts are written before native
provisioning and updated during execution. They contain exact PID/start-time
identities; they never contain credentials. Cleanup verifies exact Docker IDs,
run labels and database bind mounts, preserving the underlying database files.
`verified-inventory-stopped` covers observed descendants and processes with
verified run bindings. This environment has no per-arm cgroup containment, so
processes that discard every ownership binding between scans cannot be ruled out
universally. Remote model requests also cannot be cancelled reliably: unknown
usage retains its full accounting bound.

The phase journal records real UTC and monotonic start/end boundaries for source
preparation, initialization, probe, each batch through native readiness, input
audit and shutdown. Crashes leave an explicitly unfinished phase. Metrics retain
every observed request ID, request-body hash, timing, HTTP status, generation ID,
token counts/details and reported charge or unresolved bound. Identical bodies
are shown without assuming they constitute retries. Admission-to-response time
includes network, model processing and provider queueing; remaining time cannot
be separated reliably into local work, polling and harness overhead. Local
compute and local embedding cost are excluded. Concurrent trials do not support
an intrinsic latency ranking. Full source and stored-content review remains a
separate gate after processing; full-week stores cannot feed earlier benchmark
checkpoints.

OpenViking resource acceptance can precede detached ancestor summaries. The
import adapter uses its documented global wait followed by a healthy idle
queue observer (including Semantic-Nodes), under the same external deadline.
It also reads native summary artifacts for unresolved failure placeholders: a
model error can be swallowed by OpenViking while queue error counters stay zero.
Reviewed OpenRouter imports additionally reject native leaf/partial/merge
failure log events and require request-bound evidence of complete, nonempty
assistant text with a stop finish reason. This evidence stores lengths and
fingerprints, never assistant or reasoning text. The strict model-response
witness currently requires the reviewed OpenRouter transport; another transport
cannot silently claim the same qualification. These are read-only readiness
checks; no flush, fact repair or inference-setting change is applied. Failed attempts and full uncertain charge bounds are retained.

Direct accounting also retains exact failed-response bodies under the private
task state's `response-errors/`, with a SHA-256, byte count and explicit
body-complete flag. Only allowlisted retry/rate-limit and correlation headers
are retained; authorization and cookie headers are excluded. Closing or
interrupting a response does not establish that its body was complete. Native
SDK retries can replace the original exception, so inspect this preserved
evidence when diagnosing a rejection. Evidence retention never automatically
releases a cost hold or treats missing tokens as zero.

`vendor/runtime-locks` also constrains the reviewed backend dependency versions
and exact Python patch versions. Hermes and Honcho keep their upstream source
lockfiles; the extra Hermes/native SDK install is constrained too. CPU PyTorch
is selected by its exact reviewed wheel version. MemOS uses the committed npm
lock with `npm ci`, followed by its two pinned Linux text-embedding addons.
The constraint files do not install development or unselected optional packages.
`bootstrap --check` compares installed constrained dependencies and npm packages
with this manifest; a reviewed cohort now checks this before any paid work.
This prevents a fresh session from silently selecting newer transitive SDKs.

Reviewed import-only route comparisons use an explicit profile; the default
remains `deepseek-flex`. `PACBENCH_OPENROUTER_PROFILE=luna-bedrock-global`
selects exactly `openai/gpt-6-luna` on `amazon-bedrock/global`, with fallback
disabled. Register an explicitly approved additional route against the original
private accounting using `python -m pacbench.openrouter_authorize_route
--authorization '<actual user approval>'` under that profile. Registration keeps
historical rows, their original pricing and holds, the original route, and the
shared $20 cap. It does not release allocations. Bounds cover every catalog price
tier and additive cache writes. The Luna grant rejects driver/judge requests.

The measured launcher checks positive remaining headroom instead of imposing
an arbitrary dollar or maximum-output minimum. Native requests have different
output caps; only their actual admission can determine the correct reservation.
Every admission atomically checks all historical liabilities and concurrent
reservations, and refuses an unaffordable request before sending it. Native
output limits and reasoning settings are not changed by accounting bounds.

`pacbench.measured_import --providers cognee --probe-source-ids <four IDs>`
selects one existing conversation from one daily visibility checkpoint, using a
fresh store. It is explicitly a four-message probe, never a full Week result.
Default runs retain all 125 frozen messages. Cognee writes record the actual
unchanged multipart body privately, document/message counts and source hashes;
provider model requests are captured privately under each arm's
`memory-api-inputs/`, without authorization headers. These traces are excluded
from Git. The frozen source itself is not rewritten.

OpenViking's optional `PACBENCH_OPENVIKING_IMPORT_CONDITION=luna-budget-serial-v1`
sets its documented `vlm.max_concurrent=1` and records a condition sidecar. This
is a separately labeled budget-limited condition, distinct from the native
32-slot default. It does not alter prompts, reasoning, output caps or readiness.
Use it only for the approved Luna route. Performance comparisons must disclose
this concurrency difference and the simultaneous model/route change.

`PACBENCH_LUNA_CONCURRENCY_CONDITION=luna-budget-serial-v1` similarly sets
Hindsight's documented `HINDSIGHT_API_LLM_MAX_CONCURRENT=1` or Memcal's
`MEMCAL_MAX_PARALLEL=1`, only for a selected Hindsight/Memcal Luna arm. The
condition sidecar records the exact native environment. These are separately
labeled budget-limited conditions; other providers and native defaults are
unchanged. No reasoning effort or output cap is injected.

For future Luna calls only, `PACBENCH_RESERVATION_POLICY=shared-context-v1`
uses the documented shared input/output context window instead of reserving a
full input window and maximum output simultaneously. It maximizes charge over
`input + output <= context`, retaining worst tier prices and additive cache-write
cost. It does not alter output limits, reasoning, requests or any historical
hold. Every new ledger row records its policy. The public catalog's922,000
max-prompt field is not assumed to be an independently enforced cap. Actual
reported charges above any bound are recorded in full and stop new admission.

### Split roles for a scored Pizza Week

The explicit `--role-routes luna-memory-deepseek-v1` condition keeps stock
Hermes answering and the date-scoped judge on the existing DeepSeek/DeepInfra
Flex route, while all provider-native model work uses Luna/Bedrock Global.
Selection follows each persistent `-memory`, `-driver`, or `pacbench-judge`
accounting capability. Native retrieval, post-answer processing and background
workers therefore retain the memory route. No temporary global profile switch,
extra prompt instructions, output cap or reasoning setting is added. Judge low
reasoning and the existing 600-second judge/recall deadlines remain explicit
launcher options. Hindsight and Memcal use the measured concurrency1 condition;
OpenViking uses its separately recorded concurrency1 condition. Honcho retains
the documented simulated clock and native thinking-none condition.

Use the DeepSeek driver profile for the parent process. The split condition is
opt-in; older callers keep their original single profile. Both profiles must
already be explicitly authorized against the same private ledger. The installed
Luna grant remains memory/import-only; it is not broadened to allow Luna judges
or Hermes requests. Dependencies and model access still require normal
preflight checks. Fresh stores and chronological daily source gates are required
for the frozen37 questions; whole-Week qualification stores cannot answer early
checkpoints fairly.

A split scored cohort requires an explicit liability envelope. The example below
is a **no-spend preflight**, and currently reports insufficient shared headroom:

```sh
PACBENCH_OPENROUTER_PROFILE=deepseek-flex \
python3 scripts/run_reviewed_week_cohort.py \
  --preflight-only --role-routes luna-memory-deepseek-v1 \
  --cohort-spend-limit-usd 8 --concurrency 1 \
  --output runs/pizza-week-scored-luna-memory
```

The additional cohort ceiling covers settled charges, uncertain calls and live
reservations across all roles. It never raises the task cap or releases old
holds. Child runs inherit one cohort identity; previously accounted cohort
spend is subtracted from its remaining envelope rather than funded twice.
Refusal at either ceiling stops admission and retains unresolved exposure.
The historical task cap remains$20; any increase requires explicit approval
and a separately reviewed accounting-authorization update before execution.

For an estimate from existing private telemetry, without inference or ledger
changes, use `python3 -m pacbench.scored_budget_plan`. This reports scenario
estimates separately from transient reservations and a bounded financial plan;
it does not promise full completion or authorize spending. It needs the private
run artifacts and original ledger, which bootstrap does not reconstruct.

For the separately requested all-Luna scored condition, select
`PACBENCH_OPENROUTER_PROFILE=luna-bedrock-global` and
`--role-routes luna-all-roles-v1`. All three accounting roles then use exactly
`openai/gpt-6-luna` on `amazon-bedrock/global`, without provider fallback.
Role labels, chronological source gates and measured native concurrency remain
separate and unchanged. This explicitly changes the answer/judge model from the
mixed condition; it does not change the existing judge schema, low reasoning,
scoring rules, or native output settings.

`python3 -m pacbench.scored_budget_plan --all-luna` reprices historical scoring
token samples for planning. It is not a live Luna scoring measurement. The
current proposed financial envelope is14USD; it is not an approved increase:

```sh
PACBENCH_OPENROUTER_PROFILE=luna-bedrock-global \
python3 scripts/run_reviewed_week_cohort.py \
  --preflight-only --role-routes luna-all-roles-v1 \
  --cohort-spend-limit-usd 14 --concurrency 1 \
  --output runs/pizza-week-scored-all-luna
```

The existing Luna grant is import-only. After funding is established, the explicit
route registration supports `--scope scored-week-roles` with the user's recorded
all-role authorization; it preserves prices, token bounds, historical rows and
the task cap and records the scope transition. A mere role environment variable
cannot broaden a grant. Until the financial preflight passes, do not start paid
work. The current20USD aggregate cap and historical holds remain intact.
